          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Peace is divinely restored wholeness and right relationship, centered on reconciliation with God through Christ and extending to inward, communal, and eschatological harmony; it is more than absence of conflict.\",\"book_summary\":\"Peace is covenant wholeness restored through righteousness, involving completion, restoration, and repayment rather than a mere truce or subjective feeling; proclaimed wholeness under a violated covenant is counterfeit peace.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Restored wholeness and right relationship with God, others, and creation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Restored covenant wholeness produced and maintained through righteousness.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts genuine peace to covenant order grounded in righteousness and reclassifies a truce, emotional calm, or a declaration of well-being without covenant faithfulness as insufficient or counterfeit. The consensus admits inner tranquility and cessation of conflict as genuine dimensions of peace when situated within the broader divine gift, without always making covenant keeping an explicit condition.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction over the central identification of peace as restored wholeness rather than mere absence of conflict. The disagreement concerns whether covenant righteousness is a defining condition and whether some psychological or outwardly peaceful cases qualify independently.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus referent—restored wholeness and right relationship—but gives it a more restrictive covenantal and righteousness-based boundary. Its counterfeit-peace motif is already recognized by the consensus, while its emphasis on completion and repayment adds precision rather than replacing the referent. Because it excludes or reclassifies some cases that the broader consensus may call peace, the relationship is refined rather than a match.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The case for MATCH is substantial because the consensus already defines peace as holistic right relationship, rejects reduction to mere calm or absence of conflict, and recognizes false peace. However, the book's defining restriction to covenant wholeness through righteousness, together with its completion/repayment language, is more specific than the cited consensus core; the printed texts directly support righteousness, covenant peace, restoration, and counterfeit peace, but do not clearly establish repayment as an essential meaning.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
